Field self-concat through a pointer
===================================

When a function holds a `*Struct` parameter and assigns a
new value to one of its string fields where the RHS reads
the same field, the codegen must evaluate the RHS *before*
decrementing the old buffer's refcount. The two operations
in the wrong order produce a textbook use-after-free: the
old buffer is freed, then the RHS reads from it for the
concat.

The failure was silent on the bare-metal runtimes — they
ship a bump allocator whose `free()` is a no-op, so the
old buffer's bytes survive. On any real allocator
(llvm-host, trisc's bump+recycle) the freed memory is
either zeroed out or immediately reused by the next
malloc — and the concat reads garbage.

This test pins the contract directly, without involving
`defer`. The earlier `defer_lifo` test exercised the same
codegen path because each deferred body lowers to a
field-assign of the same shape, but the surface is broader
than defer — *any* `t.field = t.field op ...` through a
pointer triggers it.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct Bag
        s: string

    append_to_bag(b: *Bag, suffix: string) -> unit
        b.s = b.s + suffix

    #test
    test_three_appends_through_pointer() -> unit
        var b = Bag("a")
        append_to_bag(&b, "b")
        append_to_bag(&b, "c")
        append_to_bag(&b, "d")
        assert(b.s == "abcd", s"expected 'abcd', got '${b.s}'")

    #test
    test_concat_with_long_strings() -> unit
        // Longer strings make malloc-recycle more likely to scribble: each
        // concat allocates a new buffer and frees the previous one. If the
        // RHS reads the freed buffer for memcpy, content is corrupted.
        var b = Bag("hello")
        append_to_bag(&b, ", ")
        append_to_bag(&b, "world")
        append_to_bag(&b, "!")
        assert(b.s == "hello, world!", s"expected 'hello, world!', got '${b.s}'")

    #test
    test_inline_self_concat() -> unit
        // Same pattern but with the assignment inline (no helper). Pins the
        // codegen path for `t.s = t.s + lit` directly.
        var b = Bag("")
        // Use a local pointer so the assignment goes through `*Bag` semantics.
        var p: *Bag = &b
        p.s = p.s + "x"
        p.s = p.s + "y"
        p.s = p.s + "z"
        assert(b.s == "xyz", s"expected 'xyz', got '${b.s}'")
